rivie@localhost.localdomain wrote:
> 

> Probably not. The DEC drives change speeds; low-density floppies
> (like the RX50s) are read at 300 RPMs while high-density floppies
> are read at 360 RPMs. Normal AT drives do not change speed; all
> floppies are read at 360 RPMs. This pushes the 250KHz data rate
> of the low-density floppies up to 300KHz, which the data separator
> must support. Since the DEC data separator was designed to work with
> a drive that changes speed, it probably won't do 300KHz.
> 

Hmm, not that I'd swear by it, but this seems to be twice confused. 

Normal (i.e. SD and DD) 5" floppies used to rotate at 360 rpm. The big
8" floppies used to rotate at 300 rpm. So, in order to (almost) use the
same data separators, the AT floppy drives used 300 rpm for HD, such
that these drives were compatible with the 8" ones - but only when using
HD media. There might be one of the 36 pins for this, and/or a jumper to
fix HD mode vs. switch modes.

As it is possible to read RX50 floppies with a AT drive (yep, done it
with PUTR), I don't actually see a problem here. Or do the RX50 really
rotate at 300rpm ??

