In article <3A772E0D.59E2@c-lab.de>, Michael Joosten wrote:
>Hmm, not that I'd swear by it, but this seems to be twice confused. 
>
>Normal (i.e. SD and DD) 5" floppies used to rotate at 360 rpm. The big
>8" floppies used to rotate at 300 rpm.

No, you have that backwards. 8" floppies rotate at 360 RPM. 5.25" low
density and 3.5" floppies rotate at 300RPM; that's why 1.44MB 3.5"
floppies have 1.44MB instead of 1.2MB. Since the 3.5" floppies rotate
slower than the 5.25" high-density floppies, there's more time available
for extra data in each track.

>There might be one of the 36 pins for this, and/or a jumper to
>fix HD mode vs. switch modes.

The drives DEC uses switch based on the Reduced Write Current signal
(pin 2, IIRC). PC drives don't switch speeds, and these days it seems
like you're lucky to get one that you can jumper to a drive select other
than 1 let alone jumper to do exotic things like change speeds.
>
>As it is possible to read RX50 floppies with a AT drive (yep, done it
>with PUTR), I don't actually see a problem here. Or do the RX50 really
>rotate at 300rpm ??

The RX50s rotate at 300RPM, just like all other 5.25" LD drives. You can
read them in an AT because the AT data separator supports the 300KHz
data rate, which is what you get when you spin a 250KHz floppy faster.
