>I'd say TELNETSYM hands down. No extra UCX service to setup and fiddle with.
>We have scores of printers running on TELNETSYM - never had any need to setup
>LPD.

It's been a few years since I've setup printers with either, but isn't
there considerable extra flexibility with LPD?  You can have printers
that are actually managed on other LPD supporting machines (Windows NT,
Unix) interchangeably with those that are  managed by your VMS 
machines.  Also, other OS's that support lpr (the client side of LPD) 
could print to your VMS attached printers with LPD.

I never had any problem with the UCX LPD service.

I didn't want to comment as I thought someone with more recent 
experience would pipe up here.

Of course, YMMV.  If you don't have any printers managed by these other
OS's, and this includes locally attached (LPT1:) to Windows NT boxes,
nor anyone who might want to print via lpr from other platforms, then 
TELNETSYM may well be the only thing you'll ever need. 

In most places, I could see having both.  TELNETSYM is simpler to setup,
but LPD has more flexibility.

