Here's a real life example of how I will judge it. We use Alpha/NT for
one thing only: to run ANSYS. In certain large scale models an EV6 500
Mhz Alpha performs like a 2 Ghz Pentium 4.

We won't be buying IA64 desktops in the forseeable future but we will
benchmark an IA64 with Ansys as soon as one is available. Now I guess
it's the case that most vendors who had Alpha/NT ports will have
IA64/NT ports available close to public launch - ANSYS already lists
the IA64 version as *shipping* for both IA64/HP-UX and IA64/NT 2000.
It is the performance of these apps that IA64 lives or dies by.
Unfortunately the Ansys site gives no benchmark figures as yet -
presumably for legal reasons. Alpha tops the currently published
figures.

I personally don't give a damn about how fast it as at IA32 and
neither do I suspect do most of its initial target audience.

