I would say it's not just a matter of whether the problem is
parallelizable. Important is also the degree to which the
parallelized solution has dependencies requiring communication
and/or synchronization between the parallel tasks.
If such synchonization/communication is necessary then the
4 PIII's communicating over a network will never come close
to a 4 CPU system with a switched bus design. Nor close to even a
1 cpu design that has a significant percentage of the computing
power of the 4 metworked PC's (which the Alpha offers).

However, if you make a 4 PIII SMP System with a switched bus,
the resulting system will cost significantly more than 4
separate networked commodity PC's. It might cost as much as
an ES40, without the ES40's stellar performance!

Not every problem fits the Beowulf Cluster or SETI model.


As for the Itanium and it's long announced future dynasty.
The only reason why it might have some success, is if people
believe in the marketing. It's clear to me that Intel made
the wrong decisions with it's EPIC VLIW design. The Alpha
design team considered making an Alpha with the same design
principles back in 1993. Through initial prototyping they
decided that it's a dead-end technology, which effectively
blocks the use of known and future dynamic optimizations
for the sake of static optimizations which can never make-up
for the ability adjust dynamically to problem situation.

Note, that this viewpoint was clear to designers which already
had a relatively simple chip architecture (still without complexities
such as out-of-order processing) inwhich to base such an
excursion into VLIW design.

You can read about the Alpha design teams take on RISC vs VLIW
here...

http://www.alphapowered.com/alpha_tech_presents.html

Look for the document titled Alpha and IA-64.


For an interesting comparison of the pro's and con's of SMT,
CMP, and VLIW prlease read the recent articles by Paul DeMone
about Alpha at Silicon Insider RWT here...

http://www.realworldtech.com


I find a huge waste Intel's, HP's, and their business partner's
money, resources and talent to invest in such a poorly
considered design. Worse, is that competitors gave up
their perfectly good architectures, simply due to Intel's
marketing strength without considering the real (lack of) merits
of it's technology.

As long as Compaq continues on the course it has outlined
for Alpha, and doesn't get stuck implementing the SMT core,
there is no chance IA-64 will ever get close to beating the
Alpha Architecture. The Intel designers have
simply handicapped themselves too much with their basic design
decisions. For instance, SMT will be nearly impossible to
implement based on a IA-64 core. And, it will necessarily
run hotter with more timing hurdles than Alpha at the same
process shrink, thus limiting it's frequency range. That's one
of the reasons Itanium started with a 1Ghz 1st generation spec
3 years ago, reduced later to 800 Mhz, and now is settling
for only 733 Mhz. The VLIW design explodes the real estate
needed to implement a 64bit chip.

If Intel wants to beat Alpha, they've got to start
all over again and design a different architecture. Maybe their
marketing bozo's would call it IA-64 too, but it would
remain clear to us techies that it's a different chip family,
and that Intel failed completely.

Regards,

Keith Cayemberg


