A 60M tape nominally stores 1.3GB.  A 90M tape is 2GB.  Hardware
compressing may double these figures.  These are DDS-1 tapes.

A 120M tape stores 4GB uncompressed.  This is a DDS-2 tape.

Writing 1GB to a 60M tape sounds about right.  Some of the capacity is
taken up by various overhead in writing the records.

I don't know why you aren't getting more on a 120M tape.
 
