Richard Jordan <rjordan@mars.mcs.net> writes:
> I have a set of Digital 80 pin (160 contact) gold SIMMs; they are marked
> "32 MB SIMM", 5021275-01 B01, 5421726, screen dated 1999.  They are of
> the same physical configuration as the MS44 SIMMs for MicroVAX 3100-30,
> -40, -80, etc. and would physically plug in to those boxes (no I'm not
> going to try them!).  Any chance these work in similar vintage
> systems like VS4000-9x, or later 3100-9x systems?

  Well, 54-21726-01 is an active part with a description of "32MB TL SIMM
MODULE", at $512. 54-21728-05 has a description of "TLASER CLOCK MODULE"
so I'd guess this part is for a TurboLaser.

  I don't see any MS44 parts bigger than 16MB - all of them are composed
of 1 or more 4MB or 16MB SIMMs.

  The MS7CC-EA memory on the GS60e is composed of 32 54-21726-01 32MB
modules. The other variants of this SIMM are 54-21718-01 (64MB) and
54-24723-01 (128MB), used on the -FA (2GB) and -GA (4GB) memory modules.
Looking up the MS7CC-xx parts, they all have descriptions of "Alpha-
Server 8xxx memory".

  So it seems like you won't be able to use them in VAX systems, but
that they're somewhat more valuable than eqivalently-sized VAX memory -
perhaps you can trade with a reseller?

  The 50-series number is a part number for the PC board, not a completed
SIMM, and isn't useful here. They're generally the same as the 54-series
number minus one - is it possible you transposed the number? There *is*
a 54-21277-xx series of modules, but they all look like they're 72-pin
modules from the descriptions (8MX32, 8MX33, 8MX36)

