In article <F9Oz$AHijGq$@cartman.ourservers.net>, 
    byer@mail.ourservers.net (Robert Alan Byer) writes:

> 
> I was just given a Mylex PCI SCSI controller.  It says it's a
> Myles 960LB but when I when to Mylex's site they said from the part
> number (D040396-4E-DIG) it's a 960PU.  The only docs I could find
> were for the PD, PL and P 960 series, no PU or LB.
> 
> Anyway, can I use this with OpenVMS?  It appears to have firmware
> v3.5 loaded in it and I seem to recall some problem with v3.5 firmware?
> 

   Nope. The Alphas BIOS won't recognize the generic DAC 960. It
needs the Raid Array 230 firmware, which is a customized version of the
2.x firmware. You can't get 3.x firmware for the Alpha.

   If you really want to use this controller in a VMS box you'd have
to get the 2.x rom ( 2.x used a single flash rom, 3.x uses two roms )
It's possible Compaq will sell you one, but I don't know what the
part number would be. The other option would be to get the 2.x rom
from Mylex, then flash the DEC firmware onto it ( the firmware is on
the Alpha update CDROM ). Note this information is provided for
discussion purposes only, the end result would certainly not be
supported and the process may not be legal ( Compaq claims a copyright
on their doctored version of the firmware ).

   I think the PU is the same as the PD but supports UltraSCSI drives
(the PD just supports fast SCSI).

   The "-DIG" on the end of the part number indicates it was originally
a DEC OEM version of the controller - probably originally sold with a
Prioris server.

   I guess if you want to support large disks using a backplane
Raid controller Compaq would recommend you use an Intel box, the
controller should work fine in one of those.



