Hi,

If you boot the system, it shows the cpu type KA??, which tells you the exact
model.
I have a 3100 without model designation, and it identifies itself as KA41-E. It
is in the smaller BA42-A enclosure, which would make it an M10e. Yours is in the
larger BA42-B (I guess) enclosure which would make it an M20 or M20e, depending
on the processor. KA41-A or B is M20, KA41-D or E is a M20e. The B and E versions

are VAXServers.

I don't think you have to connect network or SCSI terminators the make it boot,
but you will get error messages. It's probably safe to ignore them.

I use a Digital RRD42 cdrom to boot, never tried anything else.
The tape drive looks like a TK50. My TK70 is twice as big. And my TK50 has a
Model TZ30 identification inside (which is basically an updated TK50Z).

Arnoud

