Wrong. IBM pulled that wonderful stunt where the boards put the Drive 
Select/Motor ON lines out in the 'opposite' sense. I have always 
presumed that this was to save them from having someone set the jumper
on Drive B! Anyway, if you don't include the twist the BIOS never sees
the drive. Withe the twist the BIOS test fails but my tool that goes 
straight to the drive manages to address drive A but not B but when I 
scan it, it looks odd.

In short it looks like its more trouble than its worth.

