I am running MLS+ 4.0d (CMW OS based on Digital Unix 4.0d) on an
AlphaServer 4100 with 2 533MHz processors, 1GB RAM, 3-channel RAID
controller, 4mm tape, DLT tape, and cd-rom.  Every piece of
documentation I've found (what little there is) sez that MLS+ 4.0d
supports SMP.

The problem is when I build the OS, add the appropriate licenses and
patches (DEC Patch Kit 1 for MLS+ 4.0d), the box works fine except that
I can't add any static routes whether it is through netsetup or from the
command line.  If anything is in /etc/routes the box hangs on bootup
right after it sez "started route daemon".  If I try it from the command
line it just hangs the DECTerm.  If I could get away with having this
box only talk on the local LAN life would be fine.  Here is the only
output I could come up with:
 
  # route -v add default nocether
  so_dat: inet 0.0.0.0; so_gate: inet 140.242.140.254; so_mask: inet
255.255.255.0; RTM_ADD: Add Route
  pid: 0, len 112, seq 1, errno 0, flags:<UP,GATEWAY,STATIC>
  locks:  inits:
  sockaddrs: <DST,GATEWAY,NETMASK>
   default nocether default

  (and here it hangs)

I did the same command on a working box and the only other line before
it spits the prompt back is:

  add net default:  gateway nocether

You can't do a ^C and you can't kill the pid for the `route add...`
process.  It's state is "U" for Uninterruptable Sleeping process.  I
don't know what the Hell it's waiting for.

I loaded Digital Unix 4.0d on the same 4100 and everything is
hunky-dory.  I can add static routes all day long.

Here's the kicker...our DEC/Compaq support for MLS+ ended on 31 Dec and
