Jerome Fine replies:

I just thought that it might be useful to ask:  "WHY?"  If the original question
has been asked just because it seems like a challenge, then definitely keep
on looking for a solution.  But since almost EVERY PC 5.25" HD drive
can be used in place of an RX50 when PUTR.COM from John Wilson
http://www.dbit.com/pub/putr/
is used or E11 and the drive is mounted as an RX50
http://www.dbit.com/pub/e11/
there is really no valid reason that I can see to attempt to use a DEC  RX50
drive directly on a PC - aside from the actual challenge of doing so which I
do regard as a valid reason in and of itself.   But if the actual reason is only
to use RX50 diskettes, then since I also have a stack of RX50 drives, I just
can't see any reason to bother when a PC 5.25" HD floppy is so easy
to find and use.

Now if the computer is a PDP-11 with an RQDX1,2,3 controller which
lives happily only with the RX50 floppy (the RQDX3 also likes the RX33),
then go for it.  But I suspect that by PC is meant one of those Intel/AMD
systems.

If I am correct, there are some restrictions to what I just stated.  Under
DOS/W95/W98 (no recommendation implied, just a true statement)
there will not be any problems.  Under NT or Linux or some other
OS which does not allow PUTR.COM or E11.EXE, I suspect that
problems will occur.  Since only a PC was specified in the original
question and since over 90% of users run DOS/W95/W98 (again
not a recommendation they be used, just a recognition that this
is, unfortunately, the situation), there seems to be a very high
probability that the target PC will be eligible to use and function
correctly with either PUTR or E11.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine



