In article <3A5E75B1.B92074F9@worldnet.att.net>, pat jankowiak <vaxhackery@worldnet.att.net> writes:
>
>I have been offered a DEC 5500 computer. I realize of corse this
>is no PC but a 'real' computer.. My question is, does it run VMS
>or UNIX? maybe ULTRIX? Is it Alpha, MIPS, or VAX? 

The DECsystem 5500 uses a MIPS R3000 family CPU set, probably
running at 25 Mhz.  It runs ULTRIX.  One of the free UNIX systems
may have been ported.  The basic system is closely integrated
CPU, memory and I/O subsystem.  It includes a Prestoserve NVRAM
and battery somewhere on the main board set.  The I/O module
include an Ethernet adapter, DSSI adapter, SCSI adapter and
glue for the Q-bus.

The system does have a VAX CPU on it, but only to run the VAX
based diagnostics.

>An R400X comes with it, but no disks. Any info on that unit would
>be helpful as well.

The box has a bunch of slots for specially bracketed 5.25" form
factor disks.  I don't recall if SCSI used a different bracket
from the DSSI disks, but either could be plugged into a given
slot and the signals would run to the appropriate bus.  The
face plate differed depending on the device because DSSI disks
had more buttons; write protect, run/stop, etc.

>I couldn't find information on this machine on Compaq's site..

Look around on ftp.digital.com.  There might be something there.

