In article <3A5E75B1.B92074F9@worldnet.att.net>, pat jankowiak <vaxhackery@worldnet.att.net> writes:

:I have been offered a DEC 5500 computer. 

  AFAIK, no such beast exists.  Most likely, this is a DECsystem 5500.   The 
  DECsystem 5500 is circa 1991.  32.5 integer MIPS, 23 SPECmarks.  This would 
  be a BA430 Q-bus (Q22/CD-series box) MIPS box.  Various Q-bus widgets -- 
  most (all?) were common with those used on MicroVAX/VAX Q-bus systems -- 
  were supported.  ULTRIX only, no OpenVMS.  The BA430 box itself can be 
  converted over to a Q22/CD-series (low-end) VAX 4000 series processor with 
  swap that includes a VAX CPU and its associated memory.

:An R400X comes with it, but no disks. Any info on that unit would
:be helpful as well. 

  Storage Enclosure.  It has two internal buses, and could be configured 
  with both DSSI and/or SCSI.  SCSI was probably the most common with the
  DECsystem 5500, but various of these systems did have DSSI.  Seven DSSI 
  ISEs or seven SCSI disks, or six and a tape.  B400X was the Q-bus/storage
  expansion variant, and provided room for ten Q-bus slots and four SCSI 
  or DSSI widgets.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
