ulmann@fafner.zdv.uni-mainz.de wrote:
> 
> Recently I got hold of a VAX-8250 which is up and running after some initial
> problems with the power supply. A few days ago a friend of mine gave me
> a KA820 CPU card as a present and I converted the 8250 to a 8350. Now two
> questions arise:

The VAX8200/8300s used KA820s, and the VAX8250/8350s used KA825s.  Mixing
KA820s and KA825s was not tested and is not supported, although some such
mixtures may work.

> 1) At the moment, the attached processor card is located in BI-bus slot B
>    since this is an address I found quite often in the documentation. Does
>    it really matter where the attached processor is located (as long as it
>    occupies not slots 0 and 1?

Any slot can be made to work for the second CPU.

> 2) Is there a real limitation to 2 CPU cards or would the machine (it is
>    housed in a 24 slot BI-bus card cage) run with three or even four CPU
>    cards?

We had a problem with 4 CPUs.  Due to a live-lock problem with the way
that interlocked queues were implemented on the BI bus, we usually couldn't
boot VMS.  Because of that problem, and the possibility that it might occur
with 3 CPUs (particularly with I/O adapters that used interlocked queues),
we did very little testing with 3 CPUs and we never officially supported
3 CPU systems.

While I would not recommend a tri-CPU configuration for a mission-critical
application, I think there is a fairly decent chance that a 3 CPU system
would work OK.

