In article <x3t66.1049$_e7.70473@news2.atl>, "Homer Simpson" <homer@burns.com> writes:
:Some models of 3100s needed a firmware upgrade to take this drive, as I
:recall.  Also, I agree that you are safer using it as a data disk.  As a
:boot disk, you start getting errors after a certain sector is reached.  Your
:disk may not be bad, just isn't getting written/read right.

  You may not get (overt) errors.  

  You will get silent corruptions of the low-numbered blocks on wrapped 
  writes (crashdumps), and not-so-silent file access errors on bootstraps 
  when wrapped-reads attempt to access any blocks of files (those blocks
  in high-numbered block ranges) during the bootstrap.

  This system is not one that has a firmware upgrade.

  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for details.

  I cannot recommend using large disks on these systems.  That said, there 
  are ways to avoid the problem if you are very careful on the low-level 
  organization of the files on the disk...  But if you (or your successor)
  messes this up, potentially via an innocent system upgrade or ECO kit...
  Again, I cannot and do not recommend using large disks as system disks
  on the affected MicroVAX 3100 series (earliest models), nor on all of
  the members of the VAXstation 3100 series.

:"Chris Scheers" <chris@applied-synergy.com> wrote in message
:news:3A5A4441.733CA75E@applied-synergy.com...
:> Hans Vlems wrote:
:> >
:> > I was given an RZ29-L drive. It is installed in a VaxStation 3100:
:> >
:> > VMS/LMF Charge Information for node HELIUM
:> > This is a VAXstation 3100/GPX, hardware model type 149
:> >
..
:> Do NOT use this disk as a boot disk on a VAXstation 3100!  It should be
:> OK as a data disk, though.

  Correct.  The VAXstation 3100 boot ROMs contain boot drivers that use
  six-byte SCSI commands and are thus unable to reference most any recent 
  SCSI disk.  This affects bootstraps (read) and crashdumps (writes) to
  these disks.  Please see the OpenVMS FAQ for details.

