In article <3A5CABF2.1CC40970@t-online.de>, Frank Themann <frankthemann@t-online.de> writes:

:The system I got is a MicroVAX 3300 Server in a BA215 enclosure...
:...used a KZQA controller someone borrowed me and tried it out...

  KZQSA.  Be aware that shuffling the number of disks on some of these 
  controllers can require reconfiguring CSR and vector settings on other 
  Q-bus modules.  (Its been a while since I've worked with KZQSA, but I 
  know KFQSA required careful checking whenever a DSSI disk was added or 
  removed, as it emulated one KDA50/UDA50 controller for each disk present.
  Changing the number of disks played havoc with CSR and vector settings 
  of any Q-bus device of less rank than the disk controller...  Donno if 
  the KZQSA had the same problem.  I do know that KZQSA had very specific 
  SCSI devices it would support, and other SCSI devices might or might not 
  work -- it was not a general-purpose SCSI connection.)

:...(with a TEAC SCSI CDROM drive)...

  See the FAQ for details -- the CD-ROM drive must have 512-byte block 
  support.  (Most will have 2048, fewer have 512.)

:....I had no documentation at all...
:...I don't even know wether I installed it correctly or not. 

  If you do not have CSR and vector documentation for every Q-bus widget
  present in the system and if you do not CONFIGURE the Q-bus every time 
  you make a change to any Q-bus module and then confirm the settings, you 
  will likely have problems.  There are a few cases of controllers -- those 
  at fixed addresses -- where you can "wing it" and can make changes without
  checking the other modules, but if any Q-bus controllers in the floating 
  addresses are installed or removed (or if a controller changes the number
  of emulations it presents), you will have to check the settings on all 
  other Q-bus controllers, and you will have to change the settings of any 
  lesser-ranked Q-bus controllers.

  Ask The Wizard has some additional details on Q-bus configuration and
  on CSR and vector settings, search for "serpentine" as a start...

:All I can be sure of is the correct 
:installation of the CDROM drive to the controller with proper termination.

  Hmm.  I've always assumed any SCSI configuration I've put together was 
  somehow wrong, even if it was within specifications.  SCSI is like that, 
  after all.

