Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> writes:
>   MSCP documentation is difficult to come by outside Compaq, with only
>   a very few manuals around.  The source listings CD-ROMs are expurgated, 
>   and do not generally include certain modules.  Among these modules are 
>   the drivers with the details of MSCP...

  Actually, DEC was nice enough to contribute a whole load of Ultrix sources
to the BSD team at Berkeley, and some of that code was used to create MSCP
and TMSCP device drivers for the later 4BSD releases. As far as I know, the
CI code was never included in a BSD release, though it was on the tape that
they received.

  The 4BSD MSCP/TMSCP drivers aren't too bright, as they roll all of port/
class distinctions up into a single driver, so there's a huge amount of
code replication between the various drivers. I also don't think they in-
cluded the code for host-based BBR (used on the RQDXn controllers).

  There are some folks who are working at implementing a full SCS abstraction
layer in 4BSD. See the page at:
http://minnie.cs.adfa.edu.au/Quasijarus/vaxhw/mscp_clu.html
They also say they have the same Ultrix source tapes furnished to Berkeley:
http://minnie.cs.adfa.edu.au/Quasijarus/vaxhw/ultrix.html

>   IIRC, the DEC 4000 series model 600 (Cobra) and model 700 (Fang) Alpha 
>   systems had separate I/O module part numbers for DSSI and for SCSI.

  I'd believe that - at the very least, the connectors are different between
SCSI and DSSI (different gender and mounting style, and one DSSI flavor uses
a different connector entirely). I'm pretty sure the bus transceivers are
different on DSSI as well.

