> IIRC, the bus length is limited to some 30 feet.
> The throughput is something like 4MB/sec (MegaBytes per second).
> It uses "node id.'s" similar to SCSI Id.'s.
> DSSI is probably o.k. for hobbyists, but too slow for today's production
> systems, IMO.

Depends on the chips. The SII was 3.5-4MB and about 30'. SHACs
would do 5 and could, with the right bits and luck, go to 60'. 
5MB/sec is slow now, but not as slow as you'd think. It will beat
fast SCSI under heavy load. Plus with SHACs, will carry cluster
trafic, and DECnet. The SHAC could have gone to a 16 node bus,
so I assume 16 bit bus as well.

There is also the NCR 710 chip in the 4000 alphas, the Cobra,
not the whitebox, that could do DSSI. They normally have HSDs
and SCSI drives.

