  A "DECstation" is usually an Intel PC (three-digit model number) or a 
  MIPS-based box (four digit model number), and is not usually assigned
  to an Alpha.  The subject line indicates a MicroVAX 3100 series, and 
  no member of the MicroVAX 3100 series system offers Q-bus expansion.

  Your reference to "big old tower" covers a number of different potential 
  system configurations and models, based on the cited DSSI series storage 
  configuration, I will guess that this system is a member of the VAX 4000 
  series, and probably a BA200-series (BA213, BA215) pedestal box.  The DSSI 
  storage configuration also exists on a couple of other boxes, and this 
  could easily also be a MicroVAX 3300 or a MicroVAX 3400 or a few other 
  MicroVAX systems (with Q-bus), and each with various wrinkles...

  If this is a VAX 4000 or MicroVAX 3300 or MicroVAX 3400 series, the usual 
  approaches for connecting a CD-ROM were via the HSD-series DSSI-to-SCSI 
  adapter -- various of the later VAX 4000 series systems included an 
  embedded HSD-series controllers -- or via the KZQSA Q-bus to SCSI tape 
  or CD-ROM (not supported for use with SCSI disks) controller.  Or via an 
  InfoServer.  Or a third-party Q-bus to SCSI controller -- there were a 
  few of these widgets around.  Alternatively, this system could be 
  configured into a cluster cluster with another system that has direct	
  SCSI support.  Or with the KRQ50/RRD50, for the connoisseur of somewhat
  older and more unusual Q-bus controllers...

  If you can be more specific, well, then so can I...  :-)

